president Chopp is ordering the Pentagon  to create a separate military branch to  handle space the president announced his  proposed space force yesterday it would  be the first new branch of the Armed  Services since the Air Force was created  in 1947 supporters of the president's  plan say the Air Force doesn't give  space the attention or money it needs  David Martin looks at what it would mean  to have a space force the Air Force  plans to conduct 50 space launches this  year and already operates 77 satellites  on orbit including these GPS satellites  which guide everything from high-tech  weapons to everyday drivers nearly  40,000 Air Force men and women are  currently assigned to space but  president Trump has told the Pentagon to  set up a new branch of the Armed  Services just for space we are going to  have the Air Force and we are going to  have the space force the American Way of  war depends on space which is why  potential adversaries like Russia and  China have developed anti-satellite  weapons a threat brought home in 2007  when China created this vast debris  field by shooting down one of its own  weather satellites General John  heightened the overall commander of US  space forces spoke with 60 minutes in  2015 it was a significant wake-up call  to our entire military until that  singular event I don't think the broader  military realized that that is something  we're gonna have to worry about Doug  lavarro is the former head of space  policy for the Pentagon it's not that  the Air Force has done a bad job but to  bring it further to bring it to where we  need to get to we really need a service  focused on it less than a year ago  defense secretary mattis wrote this  letter saying I do not wish to add a  separate service for space but now his  commander in chief has spoken although  Congress will have the final say for CBS  this morning David Martin at the  Pentagon and we'll see whether Congress  actually moves forward on this same  ambitious plan at this present moment
 

